      "description": "LLM Paraphrase attack applied — the model rewrites its own output to remove the watermark.",
      "text": "Explain what a zero-knowledge proof is and give a simple analogy. A zero-knowledge proof is a fundamental cryptographic protocol that enables a prover to demonstrate knowledge of certain information to a verifier without disclosing the information itself. Consider an analogy: imagine someone claims they can solve a jigsaw puzzle. To prove this without showing the completed picture, they could let you pick any piece and describe what neighbors it, repeatedly, until you are convinced they truly have the solution. The key insight is that the verifier gains certainty without gaining the secret. Modern applications include privacy-preserving transactions and identity verification.",
